            <ArticleTitle>[Pseudo-Wellens syndrome in a patient with vasospastic angina].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>79-81; discussion 82</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Wellens syndrome is characterised by negative or biphasic T waves in V2-V4 leads and critical stenosis of proximal part of the left descending coronary artery. These ECG changes without atherosclerotic changes in coronary angiography, i.e. coronary artery spasm are called pseudo-Wellens syndrome. We describe a patient with acute coronary syndrome and pseudo-Wellens syndrome as a cause of vasospastic angina. These ECG abnormalities need differentiation with acute pulmonary embolism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
